  Rule: M8. A reference version's description is written only if the pod lacks it

    Example: a reference series or version is described only by the first step that finds the pod lacking it
      Given a new pod for Dev on 2026-04-01 at 09:00
      And Dev enters "pairs" on 2026-04-02 at 09:00
      And the matcher runs on the records of that entry on 2026-04-02 at 09:05 (match-pairs)
      And version "2" of "SNOMED CT to RxNorm ingredient map" arrives on 2026-04-03 at 09:00 (new-map)
      And Dev enters "later" on 2026-04-04 at 09:00
      When the matcher runs on the records of that entry on 2026-04-04 at 09:05
      Then "match-pairs" wrote these reference descriptions:
        | reference                                    |
        | Cascade matcher rules                        |
        | Cascade matcher rules version 1              |
        | SNOMED CT to RxNorm ingredient map           |
        | SNOMED CT to RxNorm ingredient map version 1 |
        | CVX vaccine group table                      |
        | CVX vaccine group table version 1            |
      And "new-map" wrote these reference descriptions:
        | reference                                    |
        | SNOMED CT to RxNorm ingredient map version 2 |
      And that step wrote these reference descriptions:
        | reference |

  Rule: M9. If the pod already holds a judgment of that name, nothing is written
    Not even the reference descriptions it would have written. So running again is always safe.

    Background:
      Given a new pod for Cleo on 2026-03-01 at 09:00
      And Cleo files the judgment "about-p" on 2026-03-01 at 09:05
      And the export "first-export" is imported on 2026-03-02 at 10:00
      And Cleo enters "entry" on 2026-03-03 at 08:00
      And the matcher runs on the records of that entry on 2026-03-03 at 08:01

    Example: running the matcher again on the same step writes nothing
      When the matcher runs on the records of "entry" on 2026-03-03 at 09:00
      Then that step wrote no file

    Example: running the matcher again leaves each judgment it made at the time it was first made
      When the matcher runs on the records of "entry" on 2026-03-03 at 09:00
      Then the matcher's judgments holding allergy SNOMED 373270004 are:
        | justification    | members                                                    | at                  |
        | same mapped code | allergy RxNorm 7980 from draft 4, allergy SNOMED 373270004 | 2026-03-03 at 08:01 |
        | same mapped code | allergy RxNorm 7980 from draft 5, allergy SNOMED 373270004 | 2026-03-03 at 08:01 |

  Rule: M10. A table's current version is the one the pod names, and otherwise the one its series ships with
    rec:shipsWith in references.ttl.

    Example: a table's current version is the one its series ships with until the pod names one, though a later one is listed
      Given a new pod for Dev on 2026-04-01 at 09:00
      And Dev enters "pairs" on 2026-04-02 at 09:00
      And the matcher runs on the records of that entry on 2026-04-02 at 09:05
      And version "2" of "SNOMED CT to RxNorm ingredient map" arrives on 2026-04-03 at 09:00
      And Dev enters "later" on 2026-04-04 at 09:00
      When the matcher runs on the records of that entry on 2026-04-04 at 09:05
      Then the matcher's judgments holding allergy SNOMED 373270004 and allergy SNOMED 91936005 are:
        | justification    | members                                                                | used                                                                                                                                                                            | name                                          |
        | same mapped code | allergy RxNorm 7980 from draft 13 of "pairs", allergy SNOMED 373270004 | Cascade matcher rules version 1, SNOMED CT to RxNorm ingredient map version 1, version 1 of allergy RxNorm 7980 from draft 13 of "pairs", version 1 of allergy SNOMED 373270004 | urn:uuid:fb04c4d1-c902-86ae-969f-2454e9345ad2 |
        | same mapped code | allergy RxNorm 733 from draft 1 of "later", allergy SNOMED 91936005    | Cascade matcher rules version 1, SNOMED CT to RxNorm ingredient map version 2, version 1 of allergy RxNorm 733 from draft 1 of "later", version 1 of allergy SNOMED 91936005    | urn:uuid:74f606fe-da57-8931-9f53-0140e66a9121 |

  Rule: M11. A rule list whose row names a query that does not hash to the row's rec:queryHash refuses the run
    It writes nothing. So does a rule list with a row naming a file outside queries/v1-draft/matcher/, a row without
    exactly one rec:justifiedAs, rec:query and rec:queryHash, or two rows with one justification. A rule list's rows are
    never edited: a changed query is a new version of the rule list, revising the old.

    Example: a run whose rule list gives same-code.rq a hash its bytes do not have is refused, though two allergies share a code
      Given a new pod for Finn on 2026-06-01 at 09:00
      And Finn enters "same-code-entry" on 2026-06-07 at 09:00
      When the matcher runs on the records of that entry on 2026-06-07 at 09:05
      Then that step is refused
      And that step wrote no file
